#
# Every line below is one FINGERPRINT: commit, file, rule and line. That is the
# narrowest exception Gitleaks offers. It suppresses one known finding in one
# frozen commit and nothing else -- not the rule, not the file, not the
# directory, not the next line of the same file, and not the same value if it
# ever appears again anywhere else. A new secret is still a build failure.
#
# There are exactly three, and all three are in HISTORY rather than in the
# working tree. The current tree is clean on its own: each fixture below has
# been rewritten at HEAD so it no longer resembles a credential. These entries
# exist only because `gitleaks git` scans every commit, and the commits that
# introduced the original values cannot be changed without rewriting published
# history -- which is warranted for a leaked credential and not for a test
# fixture that was never one.
#
# Adding to this file is a security decision. Before adding a line, establish
# that the value cannot authenticate to anything, say so here, and fix the
# working tree so the finding does not recur.

